In HVAC and plumbing, the phone is the sales channel. A homeowner with no heat or a leaking pipe calls the first company they find, and if nobody answers, they call the next one. Most of those calls come when the team is on a job, under a sink or driving between sites.

An AI receptionist is built for exactly that gap. Here is what it costs, how the call should flow for a trades business, and how to set one up so it helps instead of annoying callers.

## What it costs

There are two ways to get one.

**Off-the-shelf AI receptionist services.** For most small businesses these run [roughly $30 to $300 a month](https://www.myaifrontdesk.com/blogs/how-much-does-an-ai-receptionist-cost-a-straight-answer-for-2026), depending on call volume and features. For comparison, the same source puts an in-house receptionist at $2,000 to $4,000 or more a month. Check whether you pay per minute, per call or a flat fee, because the same month of calls can cost very different amounts on different plans.

**A custom build** on a voice platform such as Vapi or Retell, connected to your own phone number, calendar and job system. You pay a setup fee plus usage, mainly per-minute voice and AI costs. It costs more upfront but follows your exact process, which matters when you need emergency rules, service areas and dispatch.

Small teams with simple needs often start with an off-the-shelf service. Businesses with emergency call-outs, several technicians or a field service tool usually need a custom flow.

## The call flow that works for trades

**1. Answer fast and say who you are.** "Thanks for calling Smith Heating and Plumbing, this is the virtual assistant. How can I help?" Callers should know they are speaking to an AI.

**2. Find out if it is an emergency.** No heat in winter, no cooling in a heatwave, an active leak, a gas smell. Gas smells should always get a fixed safety message telling them to leave the property and call the gas emergency line, never troubleshooting.

**3. Emergencies go to a human.** The agent collects name, address, phone and the problem in a few questions, then texts or calls the on-call technician straight away, and tells the caller when to expect a call back.

**4. Routine jobs get booked.** For servicing, quotes and non-urgent repairs, the agent checks your service area by postcode or ZIP, offers two available slots and confirms by text.

**5. Common questions get short answers.** Opening hours, areas covered, call-out fee if you publish one, whether you service a brand. Only from a list you approve.

**6. Everything else becomes a message.** Billing questions, complaints and anything unclear: take details, promise a call back, and send it to the office.

## Setup checklist

- Your business details, service areas and opening hours written down clearly
- An emergency definition and the exact on-call contact for each time of day
- A list of approved answers, and things the agent must never promise (prices for jobs it has not seen, arrival times it cannot guarantee)
- Calendar or field service tool connection for bookings; how well this works depends on the tool, so check it before you commit
- Call forwarding: after a few rings during the day, all calls after hours
- Text confirmations to the caller and job summaries to your team
- Call recordings and transcripts you review for the first two weeks

## Mistakes to avoid

- **Letting it diagnose problems.** It should collect information, not tell people how to fix a boiler.
- **No human escalation.** If a caller asks for a person and nothing happens, it is worse than voicemail.
- **Never reviewing calls.** The first two weeks of transcripts show you what to add or tighten.
- **Not measuring it.** Track calls answered, jobs booked and emergencies dispatched. That is how you know it pays for itself.

## Is it worth it?

If you miss more than a few calls a week and an average job is worth a few hundred dollars, one extra booked job a week usually covers the cost. Start with after-hours calls, check the transcripts, and expand once you trust it.

